(laughs).

00:00:02.166 --> 00:00:05.333
(crowd cheering)

00:00:07.208 --> 00:00:10.125
Which, ironically, is actually
filmed early in the morning.

00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:10.959
- Yes.

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:12.125
- We won't talk
about that right now.

00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:13.708
- They all are.
- Are they really?

00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:14.834
- Yeah, I've been on all of
them,

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:15.750
they're all at
2:30 in the afternoon.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:16.792
- So I don't even
feel weird anymore.

00:00:16.792 --> 00:00:19.542
- Nope, this is normal.
- Well, this is GaryVee.

00:00:19.542 --> 00:00:21.875
This is a big moment for us,
we've been waiting for you.

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:22.917
- Thank you.

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.417
- We've got our Idealogue
Show happening today.

00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:26.708
- Yup.
- And Gary's going to be

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:27.542
the keynote speaker.

00:00:27.542 --> 00:00:28.417
- Can't wait.
- We thought we would squeeze

00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:31.166
in a little late night taping
of you to get some of the

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:33.625
down and dirty,
you know, who is GaryVee?

00:00:33.625 --> 00:00:34.500
- Thrilled to do it.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.667
- I did bring, because
I know you're a big Jets fan--

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:37.583
- I am.

00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:39.166
- And I brought
Ryan Fitzpatrick's

00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:41.166
really bad clone double.

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:43.000
- Yes, I see him.
- He's here.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.208
His name's Mike,
and he's our sidekick.

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:48.375
- (laughs) Hey Mike.

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:50.000
- He's a huge fan of yours.
- Glad to see you, sir.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.542
- Oh, oh, oh,
very nice, very nice!

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:54.625
- Thank you Mike, thank you.

00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:56.875
- You know, Ryan's benched
this week, so I guess he has

00:00:56.875 --> 00:00:58.333
some downtime, unfortunately.
- Yeah.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.458
He's got a lot of downtime.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.708
- Hurts, hurts.
- I guess my first question--

00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:02.792
- Yeah.
- Before we get into

00:01:02.792 --> 00:01:04.417
the nitty gritty is--
- Sure.

00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:05.625
- Why the Jets?

00:01:06.792 --> 00:01:09.500
- You know, I was born
in the Soviet Union.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.959
We came here when I was three.

00:01:10.959 --> 00:01:11.959
- Are the Jets big out there?

00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:13.291
- No, the Jets are
not known out there.

00:01:13.291 --> 00:01:14.291
- Oh.

00:01:14.291 --> 00:01:17.458
- But, I came in the late 70s.

00:01:17.458 --> 00:01:19.667
You know, when I first kind
of, you know, this is the

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:22.291
late 70s, early 80s, kids
went outside and played,

00:01:22.291 --> 00:01:24.750
it was a different generation
when I first went out,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.166
I didn't, I wasn't Americanized,

00:01:26.166 --> 00:01:30.375
I didn't have that common bond
with the kids outside and,

00:01:30.375 --> 00:01:33.041
one early, you know,
one day in first grade,

00:01:33.041 --> 00:01:34.667
there was a group of
kids playing football,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.625
they asked me what my
favorite football team was,

00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:39.500
I said, I'm not even sure if
I remember if I spoke English

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.125
all that much, and they said,

00:01:41.125 --> 00:01:42.625
"Well, you're a
Jets fan like us."

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:43.959
And I was like, "Okay."

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:47.000
And basically that Sunday,
I watched my first Jets game,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.959
and I have not missed a play,

00:01:49.959 --> 00:01:53.542
a play, of a
New York Jets game since 1982.

00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:55.291
- Unbelievable.
- Yup.

00:01:55.291 --> 00:01:58.917
- Unbelievable.
(audience applause)

00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:00.542
I lived in New York
for most of my life.

00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:01.583
- Yes.
- And now I'm down in Florida.

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:02.417
- Yes.

00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:03.959
- So I'm having a difficult
time cause I was a Giants fan--

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:04.792
- Yes.

00:02:04.792 --> 00:02:07.500
- Here in the city, and
then went down to Miami,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.917
and I'm not doing
so well, either.

00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:09.750
- With the Dolphins.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:10.959
- Yup, so we've got
a lot of work to do--

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:13.000
- Yup, I understand.
- On both of our teams.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.333
- It's bad times in football.
- Yeah, seriously (laughs).

00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:17.959
You don't play fantasy
football do you?

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:19.083
- You know what's funny,
I've never played,

00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:20.834
this is my first season.

00:02:20.834 --> 00:02:24.667
Cris Collinsworth, who has
this kind of celebrity-serious

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:28.708
league, and so, I did it
for branding, to be honest.

00:02:28.708 --> 00:02:30.667
I'm five and one.
- Woah!

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:34.208
- Because like most
things, I'm very talented.

00:02:34.208 --> 00:02:35.834
No, I'm just just kidding
(laughs).

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.375
But I don't like to
play it because I think,

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:39.917
you have to understand,
my life is very simple.

00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:43.166
The health and
happiness of my family,

00:02:43.166 --> 00:02:44.750
the New York Jets,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:45.917
my business.

00:02:45.917 --> 00:02:47.417
- In that order.
- In that order.

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:49.917
And so, I'm a simple man.

00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:52.375
And the Jets are truly,
this is not a joke,

00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:54.750
the Jets are my true escapism.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.041
I take it very seriously,
but I have it contextualized

00:02:57.041 --> 00:02:59.125
of what it means in
the scheme of things.

00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:03.125
It's the one thing in the
world that I care enough about,

00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:05.291
that if something bad is
happening in business,

00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:08.583
it is not in my mind
in those four hours,

00:03:08.583 --> 00:03:10.417
and that's healthy, and
I think we all need that,

00:03:10.417 --> 00:03:13.708
whether that's reading,
meditation, traveling,

00:03:13.708 --> 00:03:17.083
I know mine's weird, but it
doesn't make it any less real,

00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:18.291
and I need that escapism,

00:03:18.291 --> 00:03:20.458
and it's tough when a
season like this happens--

00:03:20.458 --> 00:03:21.750
- Yeah.
- When a game's become

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.250
kind of not as important.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.000
- Yeah.
- I'm not as invested.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.875
But I go, and I watch,
and I support--

00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:29.500
- Yeah
- Yeah, it's a big deal for me.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.708
- Yeah.

00:03:30.708 --> 00:03:32.083
Gary--
- Yes.

00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:35.041
- What is the most valuable
asset today, in the world?

00:03:35.041 --> 00:03:36.208
- Attention.

00:03:36.208 --> 00:03:37.291
- Okay.
- Attention.

00:03:37.291 --> 00:03:39.708
If we're talking the context
of who I know is watching.

00:03:39.708 --> 00:03:42.750
Before you can tell me how
great you are at your craft,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.625
or how great your product is,

00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:47.792
or why I should come and see
you, you need my attention.

00:03:47.792 --> 00:03:50.834
And so if you're talking, in
a place where the end consumer

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.792
is no longer
paying attention to,

00:03:52.792 --> 00:03:54.583
you're in deep shit.

00:03:54.583 --> 00:03:56.625
And so when you're spending
an ungodly amount of money on

00:03:56.625 --> 00:04:00.875
direct mail, or outdoor
media, or television,

00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:04.917
or traditional digital like
banner ads, or pre-rolls,

00:04:04.917 --> 00:04:08.250
I don't think that's as valuable
a move as something like

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.750
what you're doing right now,
which is a content play,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.834
or Facebook, or Instagram,
or Snapchat,

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.792
and so, I've been pulling the
levers of attention since

00:04:15.792 --> 00:04:18.000
I was a six-year old,
trying to sell lemonade,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.166
and trying to figure
out which pole or tree

00:04:21.166 --> 00:04:24.500
to put my sign on that would
sell more cups of lemonade.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.792
I used to think about how
I would, this is funny,

00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:29.125
this is perfect, I used
to do baseball card shows,

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:30.500
and I would literally sit there,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.792
and people used to
think I had some sort of

00:04:31.792 --> 00:04:35.834
either weird disease, or was
like super, kind of like,

00:04:35.834 --> 00:04:38.208
you know, wired in a weird way,

00:04:38.208 --> 00:04:42.291
'cause I would sit and think
about the layout of my cards

00:04:42.291 --> 00:04:44.959
for two hours before
the mall would open,

00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:48.542
and then would watch how
people would look at my table.

00:04:48.542 --> 00:04:50.417
I've been following
attention my whole life.

00:04:50.417 --> 00:04:52.667
When I was dragged in
my dad's liquor store,

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:56.500
when I was 14, and started
working there, you know,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.792
there were some of the guys
that thought I was weird

00:04:58.792 --> 00:05:00.000
because I wasn't working,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.792
but all I was doing was just
literally watching the pattern

00:05:03.792 --> 00:05:05.959
of how somebody would
walk through the store.

00:05:05.959 --> 00:05:08.333
Later I understood the
biggest retailers in the world

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:11.792
had cameras following
us and understood UI/UX,

00:05:11.792 --> 00:05:14.542
and all this stuff,
or the shelf at a bar,

00:05:14.542 --> 00:05:16.041
why are those products there,

00:05:16.041 --> 00:05:19.375
but that came to me as
natural as you can imagine.

00:05:19.375 --> 00:05:22.667
It is my natural gift to
understand where consumers'

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.667
attention is, a little bit
faster than the marketplace,

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:29.583
which has been the
foundation of my success.

00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:30.792
- Crazy.

00:05:30.792 --> 00:05:33.750
Do you, how valuable
to you is time?

00:05:35.291 --> 00:05:37.208
- I mean, it's the
whole thing, right?

00:05:37.208 --> 00:05:38.667
I mean, when I--
- You are--

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:39.625
- I talk a lot about time.

00:05:39.625 --> 00:05:42.000
- I don't think I've
ever seen anyone as...

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.333
You wake up early
in the morning.

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:46.583
You do 15 minutes is
kind of your max, right?

00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:50.417
- Yeah, I mean I think, and
I don't know what else to say.

00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:52.041
I mean I think all of
us here in this studio,

00:05:52.041 --> 00:05:55.083
everybody watching at home
like, time is the game.

00:05:55.083 --> 00:05:57.041
Like you can do
anything you want,

00:05:57.041 --> 00:05:59.708
you can have all the money,
success, connections,

00:05:59.708 --> 00:06:02.208
nobody's figured out how
to add that 25th hour.

00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:05.792
And yeah, I think time
is super important.

00:06:05.792 --> 00:06:08.166
You know, Uber, my investment
Uber was a big one.

00:06:08.166 --> 00:06:09.792
I passed on Uber
in the angel round,

00:06:09.792 --> 00:06:12.208
which would've been ungodly.
- Wow, wow.

00:06:12.208 --> 00:06:13.959
- You know, really tough
to say that publicly

00:06:13.959 --> 00:06:17.291
that I left those
$300 million on the table.

00:06:17.291 --> 00:06:19.333
Not only did I pass on
it, I passed on it twice.

00:06:19.333 --> 00:06:21.083
Not only that, it gets worse,

00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:22.792
my first business
book Crush It!,

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:26.208
I thank my entire family
and one random human being,

00:06:26.208 --> 00:06:28.625
the co-founder and
CEO of Uber, Travis,

00:06:28.625 --> 00:06:31.083
who's a dear friend of
mine for a long time.

00:06:31.083 --> 00:06:33.083
I didn't understand it,
the first time.

00:06:33.083 --> 00:06:37.583
When I invested in a little
bit later, so I'll do okay,

00:06:37.583 --> 00:06:40.625
I realized, holy shit, Uber
doesn't sell transportation,

00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:43.625
Uber sells the
perception of time.

00:06:43.625 --> 00:06:46.917
And I believe that any
company in today's world

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:50.291
that takes time and
resells it back to people

00:06:50.291 --> 00:06:52.542
will build the biggest
companies in the world.

00:06:52.542 --> 00:06:55.542
And so even the way
I think about advertising,

00:06:55.542 --> 00:06:57.542
if you're trying to, if
I'm trying to watch this

00:06:57.542 --> 00:07:00.208
YouTube video but
you put a banner,

00:07:00.208 --> 00:07:02.917
a pre-roll before it
you stole my time.

00:07:02.917 --> 00:07:04.250
I came here to watch this,

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.250
not to see this
car driving up a hill.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.083
And so, time is the asset.

00:07:09.083 --> 00:07:12.125
By the way, by a small group
here, by show of hands,

00:07:12.125 --> 00:07:13.458
I know everybody
can't see this at home

00:07:13.458 --> 00:07:15.250
but I'll do color
commentary for you.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:17.125
By show of hands,
how many people here

00:07:17.125 --> 00:07:21.166
are now mad when another
human being calls them?

00:07:21.166 --> 00:07:24.125
Raise your hand.
- It's rude.

00:07:24.125 --> 00:07:25.834
It's just downright rude.
- Think about that.

00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:27.917
Half this room just
raised their hands.

00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:30.542
Think about asking that
question 10 years ago.

00:07:30.542 --> 00:07:32.291
It just wouldn't
have even made sense.

00:07:32.291 --> 00:07:35.959
We now know there's other
ways to use technology.

00:07:35.959 --> 00:07:40.000
Like, you text me, and I'll
get back to you on my time.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.750
That's why it's rude.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.125
And so time has become
the thing that we've put on

00:07:45.125 --> 00:07:48.333
a pedestal because for
some of us in this room,

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:50.291
we're not 18-year-olds,
we're not 16-year-olds,

00:07:50.291 --> 00:07:52.583
we remember the world
when it used to actually

00:07:52.583 --> 00:07:55.500
shut off at 6
or 7 P.M. professionally.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:56.834
And you'd get to it tomorrow.

00:07:56.834 --> 00:07:57.959
That world does not exist.

00:07:57.959 --> 00:08:00.291
You get people emailing
you at 11 P.M. there's some

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:02.500
sort of expectation that
you may get back to them.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.542
We live in a 24/7/365 world

00:08:04.542 --> 00:08:07.917
where we're pounded
by information.

00:08:07.917 --> 00:08:11.875
So now we, subconsciously,
we've formed

00:08:11.875 --> 00:08:15.834
into this enormous belief
and value around time

00:08:15.834 --> 00:08:18.208
and it's precious and
I do reverse engineer a lot

00:08:18.208 --> 00:08:20.750
of my strategies around
how to not steal time.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:21.625
- That was actually
one of the questions.

00:08:21.625 --> 00:08:22.583
- Which is?
- So based off this

00:08:22.583 --> 00:08:23.834
concept of time--
- Yes.

00:08:23.834 --> 00:08:25.708
- being such a valuable
asset, right, for you?

00:08:25.708 --> 00:08:26.834
- Yes.
- For everybody.

00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:28.750
- Yes.
- It helps to prioritize

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.291
what you invest in,
what you spend money in

00:08:31.291 --> 00:08:33.166
your business on,
what are the things

00:08:33.166 --> 00:08:34.457
that are going to get you
the most out of your time?

00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:35.542
- You know it's funny,
I think of it

00:08:35.542 --> 00:08:37.000
almost in reverse of that.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.207
Yes, that's all true and you
can list all the other things.

00:08:39.207 --> 00:08:42.332
It makes me
overwhelmingly focused

00:08:42.332 --> 00:08:44.750
on not wasting the
other person's time.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.875
Because then I'm
bringing the most value.

00:08:46.875 --> 00:08:48.458
The keynote I'm about to give,

00:08:48.458 --> 00:08:51.000
it's not a canned speech,
I think you know that.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.458
I know who's in the audience.

00:08:52.458 --> 00:08:54.625
You know, and
I'm gonna think about what

00:08:54.625 --> 00:08:57.291
can I talk about that's gonna
bring them the most value?

00:08:57.291 --> 00:09:00.417
Yesterday morning in
Orlando at the ANA,

00:09:00.417 --> 00:09:02.166
where it's the biggest TV
advertisers in the world,

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:03.875
I talked about something
completely different.

00:09:03.875 --> 00:09:06.875
So, that's predicated on
actually valuing people's time.

00:09:06.875 --> 00:09:08.500
I don't want them to
sit there for 45 minutes

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:11.041
and zone out,
or not get any value.

00:09:11.041 --> 00:09:11.875
They're sitting there.

00:09:11.875 --> 00:09:13.875
Even this interview,
I'm like obsessed

00:09:13.875 --> 00:09:16.667
at the highest level of, "Okay,
who's watching this show?

00:09:16.667 --> 00:09:19.792
"Got it, that demo, what can
I say that's practical to them

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:21.750
"that they can take away
and do something with?"

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.625
And I just do that
with my employees,

00:09:23.625 --> 00:09:25.792
with my customers,
with my audience.

00:09:25.792 --> 00:09:27.208
And I think that's
why I'm successful.

00:09:27.208 --> 00:09:31.125
Because if you bring more value
than you ask for in return,

00:09:31.125 --> 00:09:33.417
you win in life,
let alone business.

00:09:33.417 --> 00:09:35.208
- Yeah, I've always thought,
you know,

00:09:35.208 --> 00:09:36.500
hairdressers are interesting.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.625
And I think the service
industry as a whole

00:09:38.625 --> 00:09:40.375
is an interesting kind of beast

00:09:40.375 --> 00:09:42.166
as this whole time
and social media.

00:09:42.166 --> 00:09:43.792
And what you said, it's so true,

00:09:43.792 --> 00:09:47.375
everyone's bombarding you
for information and time.

00:09:47.375 --> 00:09:48.667
They're trying to
steal it from ya, right?

00:09:48.667 --> 00:09:50.417
Whether it's
banner ads or whatever.

00:09:50.417 --> 00:09:52.250
